## ============================================================================
## Image Registry
## ============================================================================
## OCI registry hosting the published dfe-* images. Default is GHCR.
## For cross-arch builds, set DOCKER_DEFAULT_PLATFORM.
#
# DOCKER_DEFAULT_PLATFORM=
# IMAGE_REGISTRY=ghcr.io/hyperi-io
#
## Registry pull credentials. `make login` (a prerequisite of `make ci` and
## `make stack`) reads these and logs docker + oras in, so an unattended pull of
## the private dfe-* images and the signed stack-manifest needs nothing typed.
## DFE_GHCR_TOKEN is a PAT (or GITHUB_TOKEN) with read:packages; a deployment's
## own tooling can inject both from its secrets backend at deploy time.
## Leave BOTH blank if the daemon is already authenticated out of band (an
## existing docker login / ~/.docker/config.json) - `make login` then no-ops.
## Never commit real values here.
#
# DFE_GHCR_USERNAME=
# DFE_GHCR_TOKEN=


## ============================================================================
## A second stack on this host
## ============================================================================
## A container name is DAEMON-WIDE, so the compose project name alone does not
## isolate a stack: a second one fails on `Conflict. The container name
## "/dfe-loader" is already in use`, and an EXITED container from a project
## nobody is running still holds the name. DFE_CONTAINER_PREFIX is prepended to
## every container_name in docker-compose.yml and to each per-source instance
## generated by scripts/instances.py. Empty (the default) is today's names.
##
## It covers container names and nothing else. The other two collisions are
## yours to set at the same time:
##   - networks and volumes: `docker compose -p <name>` or COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME.
##   - host ports: every *_PORT below, moved to a block of your own. Each is a
##     host-side port, CLICKHOUSE_HTTP_PORT and CLICKHOUSE_NATIVE_PORT included.
##     The two CLICKHOUSE_EXTERNAL_*_PORT are not host ports, so a second stack
##     leaves them as they are.
## Service keys are untouched, so the in-network addresses every config uses
## (clickhouse:8123, dfe-loader:6000) still resolve inside each stack.
#
# DFE_CONTAINER_PREFIX=


## ============================================================================
## Host port binding
## ============================================================================
## Compose is a supported production deploy target for small environments, so
## host exposure is split by AUDIENCE rather than left open:
##
##   DFE_INGRESS_BIND_HOST - what USERS reach. Receiver ingest (6000/8080) and
##       fetcher ingest (8082).
##       Defaults to 0.0.0.0 because an ingest port nobody can reach is useless.
##   DFE_BIND_HOST - what OPERATORS reach. ClickHouse (8123/9000), Kafka
##       (9092/19092), every /metrics port (9090-9096), the loader's internal
##       gRPC (50051), the Vector API (8686) and the collector's health port
##       (13133).
##       Defaults to 127.0.0.1: on a laptop that costs nothing, and on a partner
##       box it is the difference between an internal port and an open database.
##   The WEB UIS are the third audience and have their own section below.
##
## Remote dev hosts (a shared box you reach over the network) set
## DFE_BIND_HOST=0.0.0.0. Do that knowingly - ClickHouse ships no password by
## default, so an open 8123 is an open database.
#
# DFE_BIND_HOST=127.0.0.1
# DFE_INGRESS_BIND_HOST=0.0.0.0


## ============================================================================
## Web UI exposure
## ============================================================================
## Every web UI publishes to the host by DEFAULT. Two things control that: WHERE
## it publishes (the bind scope) and WHETHER it publishes at all (the flags).
##
## DFE_BIND_SCOPE is one dial with two modes, because "external" means something
## different on the two boxes compose deploys to:
##
##   localhost (the DEFAULT) - a developer workstation. Every UI publishes on
##       127.0.0.1, so the host sees them and the LAN does not.
##   all - a small or VM deploy. Every UI publishes on 0.0.0.0.
##
## It governs the UI ports ONLY: the UI proxy (3000), the engine API (8003),
## Kafbat (8081) and the HyperDX app plus its API (8090/8000). Ingest and the
## backing services keep their own audience above and never move with it.
##
## The Makefile resolves the scope into DFE_UI_BIND_HOST, the address compose
## interpolates. Do not set that by hand -- like DFE_DATA_ROOT, the dial takes
## effect through `make`, and a raw `docker compose` gets the loopback default.
##
## UIs carry a CLASS. PRODUCT is the DFE UI and the engine API the UI consumes;
## INFRA is the ops consoles. DFE_INFRA_UIS_EXTERNAL=false is the kill switch: it
## unpublishes every infra UI at once and beats their individual flags. The
## product UIs are exempt and answer only to their own flag, so the DFE UI never
## goes dark as a side effect of locking the ops surfaces down.
##
## Unpublishing leaves the container running and reachable on the compose
## network. It is not access control - the stack still authenticates nobody. And
## nothing here closes the engine API: dfe-proxy reverse-proxies /api/v1/* on
## DFE_UI_PORT whatever DFE_ENGINE_API_EXTERNAL says.
##
## HyperDX at DFE_BIND_SCOPE=all is the sharp edge. Next.js inlines
## CLICKHOUSE_PASSWORD into its client bundle and local mode has no login, so
## every browser on the LAN that loads that page reads the credential. Set
## DFE_HYPERDX_UI_EXTERNAL=false, or the kill switch, unless you mean it.
#
# DFE_BIND_SCOPE=localhost
# DFE_INFRA_UIS_EXTERNAL=true
# DFE_UI_EXTERNAL=true
# DFE_ENGINE_API_EXTERNAL=true
# DFE_KAFBAT_UI_EXTERNAL=true
# DFE_HYPERDX_UI_EXTERNAL=true


## ============================================================================
## External origin
## ============================================================================
## The scheme and host a BROWSER reaches this box on, with no port and no
## trailing slash -- every service appends its own port. Set it to a hostname on
## anything but a workstation.
##
## Three surfaces build absolute URLs from it and each gets the redirect wrong
## when it says localhost and the browser did not: the DFE UI's post-logout
## redirect, the oauth2-proxy callbacks the IdP must have registered, and
## HyperDX's link-backs into the DFE UI.
##
## DFE_OAUTH2_PROXY_EXTERNAL_ORIGIN and DFE_HYPERDX_APP_URL still override it
## per-surface, for a deployment that genuinely serves them on different names.
## Unset, they all follow this one.
##
## It defaults to localhost, and localhost is WRONG the moment a browser reaches
## this box by any other name -- the DFE UI has no fallback that infers the real
## one. Reaching the stack on a hostname and not setting this is what sends
## every logout to http://localhost:3000.
#
# DFE_EXTERNAL_ORIGIN=http://localhost


## ============================================================================
## Infra UI authentication (opt-in `auth` profile)
## ============================================================================
## DFE_AUTH_ENABLED=true puts an oauth2-proxy in front of each infra UI, so
## reaching Kafbat or HyperDX needs an OIDC sign-in AND membership of one of
## DFE_OIDC_ALLOWED_GROUPS. That group check is the point - authentication alone
## is not the gate. It is the docker half of the same seam k8s serves at the
## Envoy edge.
##
## OFF by default, and it must stay possible to run every other profile without
## an issuer: no dfe-docker profile may require an IdP to exist. Arming it with a
## setting missing stops `make` and names the key, rather than starting a proxy
## that redirects nowhere.
##
## Three proxies, one per ORIGIN: Kafbat (8081), the HyperDX app (8090) and the
## HyperDX API (8000), which the browser calls directly. They take over those
## host ports and the UIs stop publishing their own. One cookie secret across all
## three, and cookies ignore port, so one sign-in covers the lot.
##
## The auth profile gates PAGE ACCESS ONLY - neither UI learns who signed in, and
## neither gains a session, roles or an audit trail of its own.
##
## It does NOT contain the HyperDX credential leak: Next.js inlines
## CLICKHOUSE_PASSWORD into the client bundle, so anyone who passes the group
## check still reads it out of the JavaScript.
##
## Register all three redirect URIs with the IdP:
##   <DFE_OAUTH2_PROXY_EXTERNAL_ORIGIN>:{8081,8090,8000}/oauth2/callback
## and make sure it emits a groups claim, or every sign-in is refused.
##
## DFE_OAUTH2_PROXY_COOKIE_SECRET is generated by `make init` (32 bytes -
## oauth2-proxy accepts exactly 16, 24 or 32). DFE_OAUTH2_PROXY_VERSION is NOT
## SSoT-derived: oauth2-proxy is outside the DFE stack manifest, so `make stack`
## cannot pin it and docker-compose.yml carries the digest as its default. Set
## this only to override, and keep the tag@sha256 form.
##
## Set DFE_OAUTH2_PROXY_COOKIE_SECURE=true behind real TLS. On plain HTTP a
## Secure cookie is never sent back and every sign-in loops.
#
# DFE_AUTH_ENABLED=false
# DFE_OIDC_ISSUER_URL=
# DFE_OIDC_CLIENT_ID=
# DFE_OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET=
# DFE_OIDC_ALLOWED_GROUPS=dfe-infra,dfe-admin
# DFE_OIDC_EMAIL_DOMAINS=*
# DFE_OAUTH2_PROXY_COOKIE_SECRET=
# DFE_OAUTH2_PROXY_COOKIE_DOMAIN=
# DFE_OAUTH2_PROXY_COOKIE_SECURE=false
## Per-surface override of DFE_EXTERNAL_ORIGIN, which it follows when unset.
# DFE_OAUTH2_PROXY_EXTERNAL_ORIGIN=
# DFE_OAUTH2_PROXY_VERSION=v7.15.4@sha256:b1b2021fe8f4004573e8d690dec6c7bb29cc44364572cf8510a05bf3a0ae2ded


## ============================================================================
## OIDC test-fixture login
## ============================================================================
## The shared throwaway login every fixture account carries, so a tester who
## cannot reach the secret store can still sign in to the console through an
## external provider. Nothing in the stack reads these: they name a HUMAN test
## identity, not a service credential, and they are neither the oauth2-proxy
## client above nor the engine's own client in env/engine.env.
##
## `make creds` prints the user and names the key the password lives in. It never
## prints the password. The console acceptance specs resolve the same names.
##
## A per-provider pair wins over the generic one, so a provider needing a
## different login does not split the rest. <PROVIDER> is the provider name
## uppercased as /api/v1/auth/setup-status reports it -- DEX, OKTA, ENTRA and
## GOOGLE today. Each name resolves per-provider, then generic, then unset, and a
## provider with no password is SKIPPED by the specs rather than failed.
##
## Throwaway identities on accounts that exist for testing. Never put a login
## that works anywhere real here, and never commit a value.
#
# DFE_OIDC_FIXTURE_USER=dfe-test@dfe-oidc.test
# DFE_OIDC_FIXTURE_PASSWORD=
#
## Per-provider overrides, one pair per provider that needs its own login
# DFE_OIDC_OKTA_FIXTURE_USER=
# DFE_OIDC_OKTA_FIXTURE_PASSWORD=


## ============================================================================
## Resource limits
## ============================================================================
## Every service declares deploy.resources.limits; `docker compose up` honours
## cpus and memory outside Swarm. An unlimited service on a single box is how
## one runaway container takes the host down - ClickHouse especially.
##
## Defaults are sized so the DEFAULT profile fits a 16 GB box that is ALSO running
## an operating system and whatever else lives there - 16 GB of RAM is not 16 GB of
## headroom. Enabling every profile at once wants retuning, not these defaults.
## Run `make limits` for the actual per-service values and totals -- computed from
## the resolved config, so it cannot go stale the way a written-down total does.
##
## Limits are ceilings, not reservations - nothing is pre-allocated, so a service
## sitting idle costs nothing.
##
## Four tiers: clickhouse; broker (kafka); service (the DFE components, engine, UI,
## kafka-ui, HyperDX, its Postgres and FerretDB); sidecar (kafka-init, dlq-init,
## dfe-proxy). kafka-ui is on the SERVICE tier, not sidecar - kafbat is a Spring
## Boot JVM that sizes its heap off the container limit, so 512M OOM-kills it into
## a restart loop.
##
## HyperDX takes the service tier's CPUs and its own memory, DFE_HYPERDX_MEMORY.
## On node 24 its four Node processes hold about 820 MiB for the first 100 s after
## a start, so the service tier's 768M OOM-kills it into a restart loop.
##
## If you raise REDPANDA_MEMORY, raise DFE_BROKER_MEMORY above it. Redpanda
## subtracts the HOST's vm.min_free_kbytes from the container limit before it
## checks --memory, so redpanda/start.sh passes the smaller of REDPANDA_MEMORY and
## what that leaves, logs a WARN naming the DFE_BROKER_MEMORY that restores the
## full amount, and refuses to start under 512M.
##
## Docker allows an equal amount of SWAP alongside a memory limit, so a 3G limit
## can become 3G RAM + 3G swap on a swap-enabled host. Set memswap_limit equal to
## the memory limit per service if you need a genuinely hard cap.
##
## DFE_SERVICE_CPUS below 2.0 is not "a bit slower" - it is broken. Tokio sizes
## its worker pool from the cgroup quota and floors it, so a ceiling under 2.0
## gives a scalo service ONE worker thread, which its Kafka poll loop then owns.
## dfe-archiver at 1.5 accepted connections on :9090 and answered none of them,
## so its healthcheck never passed while the data path kept working. See the
## x-limits-service comment in docker-compose.yml for the proof.
#
# DFE_CLICKHOUSE_CPUS=2.0
# DFE_CLICKHOUSE_MEMORY=3G
# DFE_BROKER_CPUS=1.5
# DFE_BROKER_MEMORY=2560M
# DFE_SERVICE_CPUS=2.0
# DFE_SERVICE_MEMORY=768M
# DFE_HYPERDX_MEMORY=2G
# DFE_SIDECAR_CPUS=0.5
# DFE_SIDECAR_MEMORY=512M


## ============================================================================
## Generated secrets
## ============================================================================
## `make init` mints a random value for each into .env - including topping up an
## existing .env that predates the key - so a fresh checkout still just works.
## Set them by hand only if you need a specific value.
##
## These do NOT hard-fail, unlike the image pins. Compose interpolates every
## service before profiles filter anything, so a `${VAR:?}` here would abort
## `make down` and `make ps` for someone who never enabled the service the secret
## belongs to. They carry a loud sentinel default instead, and `make post` fails
## on it. So `make init` is NOT optional: copy this file by hand and you get a
## stack running a publicly committed signing key with nothing stopping you.
##
##   DFE_UI_NEXTAUTH_SECRET   - dfe-ui session signing key. Previously defaulted
##                              to the literal string "secret", and because
##                              .env.example shipped it commented out, that
##                              default is what actually ran.
##   DFE_AUTH_LOCAL_ADMIN_PASSWORD - the `admin` login for dfe-ui and the engine
##                              API. The engine issues it with a forced change at
##                              first login, and `make post` makes that change and
##                              records the new password here. Changing it by hand
##                              is a rotation the next boot issues the same way. A
##                              value the engine last issued, or the account's
##                              current password, re-issues nothing.
##                              The engine REFUSES TO START when it is empty or
##                              "changeme" unless DFE_ENV is a dev posture.
##   DFE_AUTH_BREAKGLASS_PASSWORD - the `breakglass` recovery admin. The engine
##                              hashes it into the deploy repo on its first boot
##                              and ignores it after that, so it survives losing
##                              the engine, the UI and this file. `make creds`
##                              points at it rather than printing it.
##   DFE_API_JWT_SECRET       - keys the engine's OIDC session cookie. The SECOND
##                              thing the engine REFUSES TO START on outside a dev
##                              posture: it ships a known placeholder, and running
##                              on that lets anyone forge a session.
##   HYPERDX_POSTGRES_PASSWORD - HyperDX metadata store. Not published to the
##                              host, so smaller blast radius, but a password
##                              whose value is also its name is not one.
##   CLICKHOUSE_PASSWORD       - ClickHouse default user, which has full admin
##                              (DEFAULT_ACCESS_MANAGEMENT=1). Was blank. BREAKING
##                              on upgrade - a data volume created with the blank
##                              password does not re-auth against a generated one;
##                              see docs/operating.md for the migration. Skipped
##                              when CLICKHOUSE_HOST points at an external instance.
##   DFE_HUNT_RUNNER_CLICKHOUSE_PASSWORD - the hunt runner's own ClickHouse user,
##                              dfe_hunt_runner. The engine creates the user on
##                              this password and the runner connects with it, so
##                              it reaches the data database and nothing else.
##                              Needed with an external CLICKHOUSE_HOST too: the
##                              engine creates the user there.
##
## Most are covered again in their own sections below.


## ============================================================================
## Power-on self test (POST)
## ============================================================================
## After `make dev` / `make ci` bring the stack up, a small number of uniquely
## marked events are injected at the ingest edge and traced through to
## ClickHouse. It proves the stack MOVES DATA, not merely that containers
## started and ports answer.
##
## It is opt-OUT. A self test you have to remember to run is not a self test.
##
## Transport-agnostic: it posts to the ingest edge and reads ClickHouse, so it
## behaves the same on the kafka and the kafka-less (grpc) profiles. On a profile
## with no ingest component at all (loader-only) it skips with a stated reason
## rather than failing - there is nothing to prove end to end.
##
## Run it by hand any time against a running stack: `make post`
#
# DFE_POST_ENABLED=true
# DFE_POST_HOST=localhost
# DFE_POST_DATABASE=dfe
# DFE_POST_TABLE=default


## ============================================================================
## Dev source (make dev / make dev-build)
## ============================================================================
## Component source comes via git: each repo is cloned into a managed cache and
## built at DFE_SRC_REF. Set DFE_SRC_ROOT to build your own local checkouts
## instead (the directory holding dfe-receiver, dfe-loader, ...). DFE_SRC_ROOT
## is also what `make dev LIVE=1` bind-mounts dfe-engine config/schemas from.
#
# DFE_SRC_ROOT=
# DFE_SRC_REMOTE=https://github.com/hyperi-io
# DFE_SRC_REF=main
# DFE_SRC_CACHE=~/.cache/dfe-docker/src


## ============================================================================
## DFE Profile (overrides active_profile in service_profiles.yaml)
## ============================================================================
## Available profiles are defined in service_profiles.yaml at repo root.
#
# DFE_PROFILE=


## ============================================================================
## DFE Components
## ============================================================================
## Image version pins (every *_VERSION below) are SSoT-derived PLACEHOLDERS,
## set by `make stack VERSION=X.Y.Z[-rc.N]` from the DFE stack manifest
## (dfe-infra/versions.yaml). docker-compose.yml hard-fails on an unset pin --
## there is no silent `latest` fallback -- so run `make stack` before any
## compose command. Renovate no longer manages these; the stack SSoT does.
##
## ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
## General
## ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LOG_LEVEL=info
# LOG_FORMAT=text
#
## ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
## DFE Archiver
## ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DFE_ARCHIVER_VERSION=
# DFE_ARCHIVER_PROMETHEUS_PORT=9093
#
## ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
## DFE Fetcher
## ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DFE_FETCHER_VERSION=
# DFE_FETCHER_INGEST_PORT=8082
# DFE_FETCHER_PROMETHEUS_PORT=9094
#
## ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
## DFE Loader
## ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DFE_LOADER_VERSION=
# DFE_LOADER_PROMETHEUS_PORT=9091
# DFE_LOADER_GRPC_PORT=50051
#
## ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
## DFE Receiver
## ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DFE_RECEIVER_VERSION=
# DFE_RECEIVER_BEATS_PORT=5044
# DFE_RECEIVER_GRPC_PORT=6000
# DFE_RECEIVER_HEC_PORT=8088
# DFE_RECEIVER_HTTP_PORT=8080
# DFE_RECEIVER_OTLP_GRPC_PORT=4317
# DFE_RECEIVER_OTLP_HTTP_PORT=4318
# DFE_RECEIVER_PROMETHEUS_PORT=9090
#
## ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
## DFE Transform Elastic
## ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
## Selects one compiled-in transform per instance (`source.name` in
## config/transform-elastic/*.yaml), so a second source is a second instance.
## The cisco-ios instance runs the same image and needs its own metrics port.
# DFE_TRANSFORM_ELASTIC_VERSION=
# DFE_TRANSFORM_ELASTIC_PROMETHEUS_PORT=9099
# DFE_TRANSFORM_ELASTIC_CISCO_IOS_PROMETHEUS_PORT=9089
#
## ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
## DFE Transform Vector
## ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
## 9095 is the whole metrics surface: the wrapper merges Vector's own
## internal_metrics into the scalo registry (dfe-transform-vector#70), so the
## `vector_*` series arrive on it and the exporter's 9598 stays in the container.
# DFE_TRANSFORM_VECTOR_VERSION=
# DFE_TRANSFORM_VECTOR_PROMETHEUS_PORT=9095
# DFE_TRANSFORM_VECTOR_API_PORT=8686
#
## ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
## DFE Transform Vector - the filebeat source's own instance (kafka-filebeat-vector)
## ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DFE_TRANSFORM_VECTOR_FILEBEAT_PROMETHEUS_PORT=9098
# DFE_TRANSFORM_VECTOR_FILEBEAT_API_PORT=8687
#
## ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
## DFE Transform VRL
## ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DFE_TRANSFORM_VRL_VERSION=


## ============================================================================
## ClickHouse
## ============================================================================
## CLICKHOUSE_VERSION: SSoT-derived placeholder, set by `make stack VERSION=...`
## as tag@digest. REQUIRED by docker-compose.yml (hard-fail, no silent `latest`).
## Leave CLICKHOUSE_HOST blank to use the Docker ClickHouse container.
## Set to an external host to skip the Docker container.
##
## CLICKHOUSE_PASSWORD is generated by `make init` (see "Generated secrets"), NOT
## left blank - a blank password on DEFAULT_ACCESS_MANAGEMENT=1 is a full-admin
## database anyone on the box can reach. Deliberately not listed as a placeholder
## here, for the same reason as the other generated secrets. With CLICKHOUSE_HOST
## set (external instance) the credential is yours, not ours - set it by hand.
##
## DFE_CLICKHOUSE_ENABLED=false drops the `clickhouse` compose profile so no
## warehouse container starts - the external-instance posture. Setting
## CLICKHOUSE_HOST alone does NOT do this, and does not move the loader or HyperDX
## either: the loader's ClickHouse host is a literal in config/loader/*.yaml, and
## HyperDX's seeded connection is one in docker-compose.yml.
##
## CLICKHOUSE_HTTP_PORT and CLICKHOUSE_NATIVE_PORT are the bundled container's
## HOST publish and nothing else. Every service on the compose network dials it on
## 8123/9000, so moving them (a busy port, a second stack) is safe.
## CLICKHOUSE_EXTERNAL_HTTP_PORT and CLICKHOUSE_EXTERNAL_NATIVE_PORT are the ports
## dfe-engine, dfe-hunt-runner and the collector dial on an external
## CLICKHOUSE_HOST. Unset, they dial 8123/9000; leave them unset for the bundled
## container, which listens on nothing else.
#
# DFE_CLICKHOUSE_ENABLED=true
# CLICKHOUSE_VERSION=
# CLICKHOUSE_HOST=
# CLICKHOUSE_HTTP_PORT=8123
# CLICKHOUSE_NATIVE_PORT=9000
# CLICKHOUSE_EXTERNAL_HTTP_PORT=8123
# CLICKHOUSE_EXTERNAL_NATIVE_PORT=9000
# CLICKHOUSE_DB=default
# CLICKHOUSE_USERNAME=default
# CLICKHOUSE_SECURE=false
#
## Days every time-series table keeps rows, the OTel tables included; 0 disables
## the default TTL, and a source or a dfe-schemas definition with its own TTL
## keeps it. `make init` asks once on a TTY and writes the answer here live.
#
# DFE_CLICKHOUSE_DEFAULT_TTL_DAYS=90


## ============================================================================
## Kafka
## ============================================================================
## Selects which Kafka-API broker the `make` commands run.
## Options:
## - redpanda (default): Lightweight, fits 4 GB CI runners. Source-available
##   under the BSL, NOT OSS - fine for local dev and CI under its Additional
##   Use Grant, a per-deployment licence check for a partner or edge box.
## - apache (opt-in): Real-Kafka testing, fallback, and the Apache-2.0 answer
##   when that licence check does not come back clean. Each backend now brings
##   its own topic-init service, so this path pulls no BSL artefact at all.
## Image pins (APACHE_KAFKA_VERSION / REDPANDA_VERSION) are set by
## `make stack VERSION=...` -- see the DFE Components note above.
##
## Shared dev hosts: the broker publishes to host ports 9092 and 19092. If the
## host already runs a Kafka/Redpanda on 9092 (e.g. an always-on dev daemon),
## set KAFKA_PLAINTEXT_PORT (and KAFKA_PLAINTEXT_HOST_PORT) to free ports. The
## stack and the e2e harness reach the broker over the docker network alias
## kafka:9092, so remapping the host publish does not affect them.
#
## KAFKA_ADVERTISED_HOST is what EXTERNAL clients are told to connect to on the
## host-published listener. Leave it as localhost unless the broker is reached
## from another machine, in which case set it to this host's reachable name/IP
## (and set DFE_BIND_HOST=0.0.0.0, or nothing can reach it).
#
# KAFKA_ADVERTISED_HOST=localhost
# KAFKA_BACKEND=redpanda
# KAFKA_PLAINTEXT_HOST_PORT=19092
# KAFKA_PLAINTEXT_PORT=9092
#
## ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
## Apache Kafka (opt-in)
## ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
## APACHE_KAFKA_VERSION: SSoT-derived placeholder, set by `make stack VERSION=...`.
#
# APACHE_KAFKA_VERSION=
# KAFKA_ADVERTISED_LISTENERS=PLAINTEXT://kafka:9092,PLAINTEXT_HOST://localhost:19092
# KAFKA_AUTO_CREATE_TOPICS_ENABLE=true
# KAFKA_CLUSTER_ID=dfe-docker-dev-cluster-01
# KAFKA_CONTROLLER_LISTENER_NAMES=CONTROLLER
# KAFKA_CONTROLLER_QUORUM_VOTERS=1@kafka:29092
# KAFKA_GROUP_INITIAL_REBALANCE_DELAY_MS=0
# KAFKA_INTER_BROKER_LISTENER_NAME=PLAINTEXT
# KAFKA_LISTENER_SECURITY_PROTOCOL_MAP=CONTROLLER:PLAINTEXT,PLAINTEXT:PLAINTEXT,PLAINTEXT_HOST:PLAINTEXT
# KAFKA_LISTENERS=PLAINTEXT://:9092,PLAINTEXT_HOST://:19092,CONTROLLER://:29092
# KAFKA_NODE_ID=1
# KAFKA_OFFSETS_TOPIC_REPLICATION_FACTOR=1
# KAFKA_PROCESS_ROLES=broker,controller
# KAFKA_TRANSACTION_STATE_LOG_REPLICATION_FACTOR=1
# KAFKA_TRANSACTION_STATE_LOG_MIN_ISR=1
#
## ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
## Redpanda (default)
## ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
## REDPANDA_VERSION: SSoT-derived placeholder, set by `make stack VERSION=...`.
##
## REDPANDA_MODE defaults to dev-container: Redpanda's own DEVELOPER mode
## (relaxed fsync, single core, 1 GB) chosen so the stack fits a 4 GB CI runner.
## That is not a production configuration. A small-environment deploy sets
## REDPANDA_MODE=production with real REDPANDA_SMP / REDPANDA_MEMORY, and must
## raise DFE_BROKER_MEMORY above REDPANDA_MEMORY or the broker is OOM-killed.
#
# REDPANDA_VERSION=
# REDPANDA_MODE=dev-container
# REDPANDA_SMP=1
# REDPANDA_MEMORY=1G


## ============================================================================
## Kafka UI (Kafbat)
## ============================================================================
## KAFBAT_VERSION: SSoT-derived placeholder, set by `make stack VERSION=...`.
## Enabling this (on by default) will start Kafka UI whenever Kafka is created.
#
# KAFBAT_ENABLED=true
# KAFBAT_VERSION=
# KAFBAT_DYNAMIC_CONFIG_ENABLED=true
# KAFBAT_KAFKA_CLUSTERS_0_BOOTSTRAPSERVERS=kafka:9092
# KAFBAT_KAFKA_CLUSTERS_0_NAME=dfe-local
# KAFBAT_PORT=8081


## ============================================================================
## Core DFE Components
## ============================================================================
## Image pins set by `make stack VERSION=...` -- see the DFE Components note above.
##
## ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
## General
## ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
## DFE_CORE_ENABLED starts dfe-engine, dfe-ui and dfe-proxy. It is one of five
## footprint flags -- with DFE_CLICKHOUSE_ENABLED, KAFBAT_ENABLED,
## DFE_HYPERDX_ENABLED and DFE_OTEL_ENABLED -- that OVERRIDE whatever the active
## profile in service_profiles.yaml declares. Leave them commented and the
## profile decides.
# DFE_CORE_ENABLED=true
#
## ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
## dfe-engine
## ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
## DFE_GITOPS_ENABLED gives the engine a deploy repo, which is where the
## break-glass hash is committed -- set it false and no recovery admin survives a
## rebuild. Local-only here: no repo_url, so nothing is pushed anywhere.
## DFE_GITOPS_LOCAL_PATH is where that repo lives, derived in docker-compose.yml
## as <DFE_ENGINE_CONFIG_DIR>/deploy-repo so it sits inside the config volume and
## is owned by the engine's own UID rather than root. Not settable from here.
# DFE_GITOPS_ENABLED=true
# DFE_ENGINE_VERSION=
# DFE_ENGINE_PORT=8003
# DFE_ENGINE_CONFIG_DIR=/app/config
# DFE_ENGINE_SCHEMAS_DIR=/app/schemas
#
## ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
## dfe-ui
## ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
## DFE_UI_NEXTAUTH_SECRET is generated by `make init` - see "Generated secrets"
## above. Deliberately absent here: a commented placeholder in this file is how
## the old literal "secret" default came to be what actually ran.
#
# DFE_UI_VERSION=
# DFE_UI_PORT=3000
# DFE_UI_NODE_ENV=production
#
## ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
## dfe-proxy (envoy)
## ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
## DFE_PROXY_VERSION: SSoT-derived placeholder, set by `make stack VERSION=...`.
## It is the tag for envoyproxy/envoy -- an nginx-shaped value pins a manifest
## that does not exist, so re-run `make stack` rather than hand-editing it.
# DFE_PROXY_VERSION=


## ============================================================================
## Self-monitoring (OpenTelemetry)
## ============================================================================
## The stack's own telemetry, kept off the ingest path. DFE_OTEL_ENABLED starts
## the bundled collector; DFE_OTEL_EXPORTER_ENDPOINT says where services push, and
## empty exports NOTHING. Enabling the profile points them at the bundled
## collector unless you name an endpoint yourself, which is how you push to an
## external OTLP backend instead.
##
## Every DFE service pushes on the endpoint alone, except dfe-engine, which also
## needs DFE_ENGINE_METRICS_BACKEND at `opentelemetry` -- the profile sets it.
## They all still serve /metrics for anything that wants to scrape them.
## See docs/operating.md#self-monitoring.
##
## DFE_OTEL_COLLECTOR_VERSION: SSoT-derived placeholder, set by
## `make stack VERSION=...`.
#
# DFE_OTEL_ENABLED=false
# DFE_OTEL_COLLECTOR_VERSION=
# DFE_OTEL_EXPORTER_ENDPOINT=
# DFE_OTEL_DATABASE=dfe
# DFE_OTEL_HEALTH_PORT=13133
# DFE_OTEL_COLLECTOR_LOG_LEVEL=warn
# DFE_ENGINE_METRICS_BACKEND=prometheus


## ============================================================================
## HyperDX
## ============================================================================
## DFE_HYPERDX_VERSION / HYPERDX_FERRETDB_VERSION / HYPERDX_POSTGRES_VERSION:
## SSoT-derived placeholders, set by `make stack VERSION=...` -- the fork image
## is pinned from versions.yaml (content.dfe-hyperdx) like every other image.
#
# DFE_HYPERDX_ENABLED=false
# DFE_HYPERDX_VERSION=
# HYPERDX_FERRETDB_VERSION=
# HYPERDX_POSTGRES_VERSION=
# DFE_HYPERDX_API_PORT=8000
# DFE_HYPERDX_APP_PORT=8090
## The port dfe-proxy re-serves HyperDX on, with frame-ancestors rewritten so
## the DFE UI can iframe it. This is what the UI points at, not the app port.
# DFE_HYPERDX_EMBED_PORT=8091
## Per-surface override of DFE_EXTERNAL_ORIGIN, which it follows when unset.
# DFE_HYPERDX_APP_URL=
## HyperDX verifies the engine's token (`oidc-proxy`), so the console login is
## the HyperDX login. `header-dev` takes the identity off request headers
## instead, for a HyperDX running without an engine.
# DFE_HYPERDX_AUTH_MODE=oidc-proxy
## Where the ENGINE reaches HyperDX. The in-stack service unless this names an
## external one; the profile decides whether the engine is pointed at it at all.
# DFE_HYPERDX_BASE_URL=http://hyperdx:8000
## Upstream HyperDX's usage stats, sent to in-otel.hyperdx.io -- a third party.
## Off unless you choose to send them.
# DFE_HYPERDX_USAGE_STATS_ENABLED=false
# HYPERDX_THEME=dfe
# HYPERDX_POSTGRES_USER=hyperdx
#
## HYPERDX_POSTGRES_PASSWORD is generated by `make init` - see "Generated
## secrets" above. Deliberately not given a placeholder here.
